{"id":"h16t3","name":"Stock-out Periods with Missing Snapshots","lang":"sql","entry":null,"params":[],"limit_s":10,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>The table <b>snapshots</b> has the columns <code>store_id</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>snap_date</code> (date) and <code>on_hand</code> (units in stock). Stores do not send a snapshot every day: a missing day means that the stock did not change since the last snapshot of the same store and sku. When a store sent several snapshots for the same day and sku, keep the <b>lowest</b> <code>on_hand</code>.</p>\n<p>For each store and sku, rebuild the stock of <b>every day</b> from its first to its last snapshot date (both included), carrying the last known value forward over the missing days.</p>\n<p>A <b>stock-out period</b> is a run of consecutive days with a stock of 0 lasting <b>at least 2 days</b>.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return the columns <code>store_id</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>start_day</code>, <code>end_day</code> and <code>days</code>, sorted by <code>store_id</code>, then <code>sku</code>, then <code>start_day</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p>Table <code>snapshots</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>store_id</th><th>sku</th><th>snap_date</th><th>on_hand</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A</td><td>2026-01-01</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A</td><td>2026-01-03</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A</td><td>2026-01-06</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A</td><td>2026-01-07</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A</td><td>2026-01-08</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>B</td><td>2026-01-01</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>B</td><td>2026-01-02</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>B</td><td>2026-01-02</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>B</td><td>2026-01-04</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>A</td><td>2026-01-01</td><td>0</td></tr></table></div><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>store_id</th><th>sku</th><th>start_day</th><th>end_day</th><th>days</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A</td><td>2026-01-03</td><td>2026-01-05</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>B</td><td>2026-01-01</td><td>2026-01-03</td><td>3</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 10 seconds (psql)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 1 GB<br><b>[input]</b> tables <code>snapshots</code><br><b>[output]</b> the result of one query with exactly the columns <code>store_id</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>start_day</code>, <code>end_day</code>, <code>days</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"/* Write your PostgreSQL query here */\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["jours manquants","report de la dernière valeur","COUNT cumulé","îlots de dates","doublons"],"minutes":25,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>query.sql</code>. Efface le commentaire de départ si tu veux.</li><li>Écris <b>une seule requête</b> qui commence par <code>SELECT</code> (ou <code>WITH</code>). Les tables <code>snapshots</code> existent déjà, remplies avec les données du test : pas de <code>CREATE TABLE</code> ni d'<code>INSERT</code>.</li><li>Le moteur est PostgreSQL. Si tu écris plusieurs requêtes, seul le résultat de la dernière est noté.</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Le résultat de ta requête doit avoir <b>exactement 5 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>store_id</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>start_day</code>, <code>end_day</code>, <code>days</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Nomme chaque colonne calculée avec <code>AS</code>.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>ORDER BY</code> à la fin de la requête).</li><li>Valeurs : les dates s'affichent AAAA-MM-JJ.</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 8 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"tables":{"snapshots":{"columns":[["store_id","INT"],["sku","TEXT"],["snap_date","DATE"],["on_hand","INT"]],"rows":[[1,"A","2026-01-01",5],[1,"A","2026-01-03",0],[1,"A","2026-01-06",3],[1,"A","2026-01-07",0],[1,"A","2026-01-08",2],[1,"B","2026-01-01",0],[1,"B","2026-01-02",4],[1,"B","2026-01-02",0],[1,"B","2026-01-04",1],[2,"A","2026-01-01",0]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["store_id","sku","start_day","end_day","days"],"rows":[[1,"A","2026-01-03","2026-01-05",3],[1,"B","2026-01-01","2026-01-03",3]]}},{"input":{"tables":{"snapshots":{"columns":[["store_id","INT"],["sku","TEXT"],["snap_date","DATE"],["on_hand","INT"]],"rows":[[3,"X","2026-01-11",0],[3,"X","2026-01-15",0],[3,"X","2026-01-16",7],[3,"Y","2026-01-11",1],[3,"Y","2026-01-13",0]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["store_id","sku","start_day","end_day","days"],"rows":[[3,"X","2026-01-11","2026-01-15",5]]}}],"hidden_n":8,"hidden":"","solution":""}